     "type":"reaction",
     "template_id":733,
     "children":[
        {
          "type":"mol",
          "smiles":"ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(/C=C/C(=O)Nc3ccccc3N)cc2",   // sulfonyl chloride fragment (aryl sulfonyl chloride with side chain)
          "children":[
            {
              "type":"reaction",
              "template_id":401931,
              "children":[
                {
                  "type":"mol",
                  "smiles":"ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(/C=C/C(=O)O)cc2", // unsaturated acid fragment (aryl sulfonyl chloride with unsat acid)
                  "children":[
                    {
                      "type":"reaction",
                      "template_id":659569,
                      "children":[
                        {"type":"mol","smiles":"COc1ccc(C=O)cc1"}, // 4-formyl anisole
                        {"type":"mol","smiles":"C(C(C(=O)O)C(=O)O)"} // malonic acid (SMILES: C(C(=O)O)C(=O)O)
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type":"mol",
                  "smiles":"c1ccccc1N" // aniline
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type":"mol",
          "smiles":"Cc1ccc(N)cc1" // p-toluidine
        }
     ]
   }
 ]
}

But need correct SMILES for sulfonyl chloride fragment: The fragment after breaking sulfonamide: we have aryl sulfonyl chloride attached to second aromatic ring with side chain. The original target: Cc1ccc(NS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(/C=C/C(=O)Nc3ccccc3N)cc2)cc1

If we break N-S bond, we get p-toluidine (Cc1ccc(N)cc1) and sulfonyl chloride: ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(/C=C/C(=O)Nc3ccccc3N)cc2

Yes.

Then amide disconnection on that sulfonyl chloride fragment: break amide C(=O)N-aryl to give carboxylic acid (unsaturated) and aniline. The unsaturated acid fragment would be: ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(/C=C/C(=O)O)cc2 (the side chain now is unsaturated acid). The aniline fragment is c3ccccc3N (aniline). Good.

Then Doebner: unsaturated acid fragment to aldehyde + malonic acid. The aldehyde fragment is the aromatic aldehyde attached to the sulfonyl chloride: ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(C=O)cc2? Wait the aldehyde is on the aromatic ring that originally had the side chain. The side chain is /C=C/C(=O)O attached to the aromatic ring. The Doebner modification forms that side chain from aldehyde on that aromatic ring. So the aldehyde fragment is the same aromatic ring with aldehyde: ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(C=O)cc2. However we also have a methoxy group on that ring? Actually the target ring is c2ccc(/C=C/... )cc2, no methoxy. The aldehyde we propose is 4-formyl phenyl sulfonyl chloride. But the earlier plan used 4-formyl anisole (methoxy). That may be inaccurate. The side chain is attached to phenyl ring that is part of sulfonyl chloride fragment. No methoxy. So aldehyde is ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(C=O)cc2. That's sulfonyl chloride with aldehyde.

But the Doebner template we found (659569) may involve aromatic aldehyde + malonic acid -> unsaturated acid attached to aromatic ring. So the aldehyde precursor should be aromatic aldehyde (no sulfonyl). However the template may not include sulfonyl chloride substituent; but it can handle any aromatic substituent. So we can use that.

Thus final leaf aldehyde: ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(C=O)cc2.

But we also need malonic acid: C(C(=O)O)C(=O)O (SMILES: C(C(=O)O)C(=O)O) or O=C(O)C(C(=O)O)O.

Now we have leaf molecules: p-toluidine (Cc1ccc(N)cc1), aniline (c1ccccc1N), sulfonyl chloride aldehyde (ClS(=O)(=O)c2ccc(C=O)cc2), malonic acid (C(C(=O)O)C(=O)O).

